WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:15.104
steamer bound for America was called the General Grant and would take 12 days to cross the Pacific.

2
00:00:16.185 --> 00:00:21.649
Phileas Fogg was hoping they would reach San Francisco by December 2, New York by December 11, and

3
00:00:21.709 --> 00:00:23.431
London, England, by December 20.

4
00:00:26.153 --> 00:00:29.035
According to the wager, his trip was to conclude by December 21.

5
00:00:30.772 --> 00:00:35.416
Mr. Fogg's young female companion, Aouda, felt herself growing attached to her protector.

6
00:00:36.317 --> 00:00:38.919
Mr. Fogg's silent but generous nature impressed her.

7
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.764
The more time she spent with him, the more she began to feel emotions stronger than simple gratitude.

8
00:00:45.725 --> 00:00:48.768
Her new feelings, however, did not seem to have any effect on Mr.

9
00:00:48.808 --> 00:00:53.472
Fogg. Passepartout did not fail to notice the state of the lady's heart.

10
00:00:54.873 --> 00:00:57.936
She was now as impatient as Fogg was over any delay in their journey.

11
00:00:59.008 --> 00:01:01.971
He assured her that the most difficult part of the journey had already passed.

12
00:01:03.732 --> 00:01:07.955
A railway train from San Francisco to New York and a steamer from New York to Liverpool would

13
00:01:07.975 --> 00:01:09.937
bring them to the end of their incredible travels.

14
00:01:11.278 --> 00:01:14.721
Now that Fix was out of their way, no obstacles could slow their progress.

15
00:01:15.702 --> 00:01:17.343
At least, that's what Passepartout thought.

16
00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:21.186
Detective Fix, however, was also on the General Grant.

17
00:01:22.007 --> 00:01:23.368
And he had the warrant for Mr.

18
00:01:23.408 --> 00:01:24.589
Fogg's arrest in his hand.

19
00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.252
It had been waiting for him at the English consulate in Yokohama.

20
00:01:29.416 --> 00:01:32.959
Unfortunately, now that Fogg had left English territory, it was useless.

21
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.122
Fogg could not be arrested outside English territories.

22
00:01:37.543 --> 00:01:41.746
Six days out of Yokohama, Passepartout came face-to- face with Fix on the forward deck.

23
00:01:43.107 --> 00:01:47.671
Without a word Passepartout grabbed Fix by the throat and began to punch him, to the amusement of

24
00:01:47.711 --> 00:01:49.232
a group of Americans close by.

25
00:01:50.553 --> 00:01:54.396
When Passepartout had finished, Fix stood up and looked at his adversary coldly.

26
00:01:54.416 --> 00:01:56.178
"Are you done?"

27
00:01:56.678 --> 00:01:57.138
he asked.

28
00:01:57.158 --> 00:02:01.377
"For now yes," said Passepartout with satisfaction.

29
00:02:01.397 --> 00:02:05.220
"Then let me have a word with you," said Fix.

30
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.902
"You have given me a thrashing, and I expected it.

31
00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:11.924
Up to this time, I have been Mr.

32
00:02:11.984 --> 00:02:15.386
Fogg's adversary, but now I am on his side."

33
00:02:15.406 --> 00:02:18.207
"Aha!" cried Passepartout.

34
00:02:18.668 --> 00:02:20.689
"You are convinced he is an honest man!"

35
00:02:20.709 --> 00:02:23.591
"No," replied Fix.

36
00:02:23.611 --> 00:02:25.612
"I think he is a rascal!

37
00:02:26.612 --> 00:02:27.553
As long as Mr.

38
00:02:27.653 --> 00:02:32.755
Fogg was on English ground, it was my duty to detain him until the warrant for his arrest

39
00:02:32.815 --> 00:02:38.979
arrived. But now I want him back on English soil as quickly as possible, and I'll do what

40
00:02:39.059 --> 00:02:40.419
I can to help him reach it.

41
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.300
Are we friends?"

42
00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:45.302
"Friends? No," replied Passepartout.

43
00:02:45.322 --> 00:02:47.303
"But allies perhaps."

44
00:02:49.124 --> 00:02:51.986
On December 2, the General Grant reached San Francisco.

45
00:02:53.807 --> 00:02:56.168
Mr. Fogg had neither gained nor lost a single day.

46
00:02:57.492 --> 00:02:59.133
It was seven o'clock in the morning when Mr.

47
00:02:59.173 --> 00:03:02.594
Fogg, Aouda, and Passepartout set foot on American soil.

48
00:03:04.035 --> 00:03:06.076
At the first news vendor he saw, Mr.

49
00:03:06.116 --> 00:03:08.477
Fogg inquired when the next train left for New York.

50
00:03:09.617 --> 00:03:11.558
The train was scheduled to leave at 6:00 p.m.

51
00:03:11.778 --> 00:03:14.679
so they had the entire day to spend in the California city.

52
00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.281
They hired a carriage and went to the International Hotel to dine.

53
00:03:21.242 --> 00:03:22.242
After breakfast Mr.

54
00:03:22.282 --> 00:03:26.564
Fogg and Aouda were about to leave for the passport office when Passepartout stopped them.

55
00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:30.883
"Perhaps I should purchase some rifles and revolvers while I am shopping, master?"

56
00:03:31.243 --> 00:03:32.204
asked Passepartout.

57
00:03:33.224 --> 00:03:35.626
He had heard stories of Indians attacking the trains.

58
00:03:35.646 --> 00:03:41.570
"I think it is a useless precaution, but do as you think best," replied Mr.

59
00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.151
Fogg. Mr.

60
00:03:43.191 --> 00:03:47.134
Fogg and Aouda had not walked two hundred steps, however, when they ran into Mr.

61
00:03:47.174 --> 00:03:51.197
Fix. The detective pretended to be wholly taken by surprise.

62
00:03:52.218 --> 00:03:56.592
Fix asked to accompany them on their walk around San Francisco, a request that Mr.

63
00:03:56.612 --> 00:03:57.633
Fogg readily granted.

64
00:03:59.774 --> 00:04:03.615
They soon found themselves on Montgomery Street, where a large crowd had collected.

65
00:04:05.036 --> 00:04:08.417
The crowd was marching down the street, cheering "Hooray for Camerfield!"

66
00:04:08.757 --> 00:04:10.638
and carrying large posters and banners.

67
00:04:10.658 --> 00:04:13.760
"It is a political meeting," said Mr.

68
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.361
Fogg. "We had better not mingle in the crowd.

69
00:04:16.921 --> 00:04:17.821
There may be danger."

70
00:04:17.841 --> 00:04:20.903
"Yes," returned Fix.

71
00:04:20.923 --> 00:04:24.124
"A political rally can easily turn violent."

72
00:04:25.024 --> 00:04:28.627
They headed toward a flight of stairs at the side of the street that would take them out

73
00:04:28.647 --> 00:04:29.108
of the crowd.

74
00:04:30.048 --> 00:04:32.670
Just then another group, chanting "Hooray for Mandiboy!"

75
00:04:32.851 --> 00:04:34.752
came up the street from the opposite direction.

76
00:04:36.974 --> 00:04:42.459
The cheers quickly turned to shouts, and the posters turned to weapons as the opposing sides assaulted each

77
00:04:42.519 --> 00:04:45.401
other. It was too late to escape—Mr.

78
00:04:45.441 --> 00:04:47.723
Fogg, Aouda, and Fix were caught in the middle.

79
00:04:48.724 --> 00:04:53.388
Phileas Fogg and Fix bravely protected their fair companion, jostling roughly with the fighters.

80
00:04:55.112 --> 00:04:58.134
A tough, muscular fellow with a red beard rushed at Mr.

81
00:04:58.174 --> 00:04:59.395
Fogg with clenched fists.

82
00:05:00.336 --> 00:05:00.916
Before Mr.

83
00:05:00.976 --> 00:05:05.359
Fogg could clench his own fists, however, Fix stepped in and received the punch instead.

84
00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.461
Fix fell to the ground.

85
00:05:08.501 --> 00:05:13.645
Mr. Fogg stepped over him to face his opponent, but other combatants had moved between them and he

86
00:05:13.685 --> 00:05:14.886
couldn't reach the bearded man.

87
00:05:14.906 --> 00:05:18.028
"Yankee!" called Mr.

88
00:05:18.068 --> 00:05:19.869
Fogg. "We will meet again!"

89
00:05:19.889 --> 00:05:22.351
"Englishman!" retorted the other.

90
00:05:22.371 --> 00:05:23.992
"Do you challenge me?"

91
00:05:24.928 --> 00:05:25.369
"I do!"

92
00:05:25.909 --> 00:05:26.369
said Mr.

93
00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:27.350
Fogg confidently.

94
00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:28.731
"What is your name?"

95
00:05:28.751 --> 00:05:31.574
"Colonel Stamp Proctor," said the man.

96
00:05:31.594 --> 00:05:36.578
"Mine is Phileas Fogg, and I promise you we will face each other again!"

97
00:05:37.438 --> 00:05:39.000
Then the crowd swept them apart.

98
00:05:43.423 --> 00:05:44.164
It seems that Mr.

99
00:05:44.204 --> 00:05:46.846
Fogg makes friends wherever he goes and enemies!

100
00:05:47.867 --> 00:05:50.609
I hope we don't meet Colonel Proctor again—or any Indians.

101
00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:54.192
Are you wondering why there are people called Indians in America?

102
00:05:55.116 --> 00:05:55.936
Well, let me explain.

103
00:05:56.957 --> 00:06:01.358
When the explorer Christopher Columbus first arrived in America, he thought he was in India.

104
00:06:02.178 --> 00:06:05.279
So when he encountered the Native Americans, he called them "Indians."

105
00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.901
Like Europeans and Asians, the group of people known as Native American consists of hundreds of different cultures.

106
00:06:12.881 --> 00:06:15.922
Two Native American tribes can differ as much as Asian people do.

107
00:06:17.083 --> 00:06:20.904
Native Americans have lived in North America long before Europeans invaded the continent.

108
00:06:22.024 --> 00:06:25.631
Since the arrival of Europeans, there has been a lot of fighting over land.

109
00:06:26.413 --> 00:06:29.759
So far we haven't seen any of these wars, but our journey isn't over yet!
